  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-17x & >-2{x}^{2}+45 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=6{x}^{2}-17x-45$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-27x)+(10x-45) & =0 \\\\ 3x(2x-9)+5(2x-9) & =0 \\\\ (3x+5)(2x-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059052/assets/f73c9906e6b65d1ddbda91619c7ffbb77408faa0541e2bc16565e0127d334312.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-45>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<-\\frac{5}{3}$ or $x>\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})\u22c3(\\frac{9}{2},\\infty ).$",
